From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 14:31:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] xmllite: read a document in the encoding its declaration
 names, and use the MultiLanguage object for the ones it does not know.

Word gives its readers an IMultiLanguage2 as XmlReaderProperty_MultiLanguage,
and each of the 35 times said it was ignored.  So was the encoding a
declaration names: a document of bytes was read as UTF-8 whatever it
declared, and one naming anything but UTF-8, UTF-16 or US-ASCII failed.
tools/xmlmlangprobe measured Windows 11:

- The reader knows US-ASCII, UTF-8, windows-1250 to 1258 and ISO-8859-1
  to 9 by itself, in any case; after the declaration the document is
  read in the one it names, also after a UTF-8 BOM.
- Any other it asks the MultiLanguage object: GB2312, Shift_JIS, big5,
  koi8-r, euc-jp and iso-2022-jp are read with one, and fail without
  one with MX_E_ENCODINGSIGNATURE.  A name the object does not know is
  MX_E_ENCODING.
- A document of bytes cannot switch to UTF-16, UTF-16LE, UCS-2, UTF-32,
  unicode or UTF-7, and one of UTF-16 cannot name an encoding of bytes:
  MX_E_ENCODINGSWITCH.
- A name is what ends at its quote ("utf" is not UTF-16, "iso-8859-1 "
  is WC_E_ENCNAME), and a byte that is not UTF-8 is WC_E_XMLCHARACTER
  where it is, as is any other character XML does not allow in text.

The reader now converts with the system when it has the code page and
with the MultiLanguage object otherwise (iso-2022-jp), and keeps a
character of two or three bytes that a chunk cuts for the next one.
The comments of 106a65b are in English now.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit efd7c409d52a9a619509886ffdd809ca8646cafd)

